            0.846,
            0.066
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Finding Common Ground"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 1,
          "bbox": [
            0.407,
            0.09,
            0.591,
            0.115
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "SUMMARY"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.176,
            0.132,
            0.822,
            0.169
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Last year, we looked at common multiples, and common factors, and were also introduced to the amazing world of primes!"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.176,
            0.17,
            0.823,
            0.206
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "In this chapter, we learnt a method to find the prime factorisation of a number."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.176,
            0.208,
            0.822,
            0.246
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Finding all the factors of a number from its prime factorisation is easy but quite tedious — we have to list every possible subpart!"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.176,
            0.247,
            0.822,
            0.283
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Highest Common Factor (HCF)",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " is the highest among all the common factors of a group of numbers."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.211,
            0.283,
            0.823,
            0.318
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " Every common factor is contained in the prime factorisation of the number."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.213,
            0.319,
            0.823,
            0.354
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "To find the HCF, we include the minimum number of occurances of each prime across the prime factorisation of all the numbers."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.176,
            0.355,
            0.822,
            0.392
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Lowest Common Multiple (LCM)",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " is the lowest among all the common multiples of a group of numbers."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.212,
            0.392,
            0.821,
            0.426
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Every common multiple contains the prime factorisation of the numbers."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.212,
            0.427,
            0.821,
            0.462
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "To find the LCM, we include the highest number of occurrences of each prime across the prime factorisations of all the numbers."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.176,
            0.463,
            0.822,
            0.519
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "We explored more about HCF and LCM; we discovered related properties and patterns when numbers are consecutive, even, co-prime, etc."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.176,
            0.521,
            0.821,
            0.558
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "We learnt a procedure to get both the HCF and the LCM at the same time! We also saw how to make this even quicker!"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 13,
          "bbox": [
            0.176,
            0.559,
            0.821,
            0.597
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "We learned some terms that are used when discussing mathematics, such as ‘"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "conjecture",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "’ and ‘"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "generalisation",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "’."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 14,
          "bbox": [
            0.239,
            0.742,
            0.582,
            0.799
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The largest prime found so far has 4,10,24,320 digits! It was discovered on October 12, 2024."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 15,
          "bbox": [
